Foundation migrations (T0302)

Run from services/backend after provisioning the three database roles with
infra/postgres/init/01-roles.sh. MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL must authenticate as
app_migrator. Settings loads infra/.env for ENV=local; no URL lives in alembic.ini.

    uv run alembic upgrade head
    uv run alembic check
    uv run alembic downgrade base
    uv run alembic upgrade head

Downgrade removes application schemas, including their data. It leaves the
infra-owned roles/passwords and Alembic's empty public.alembic_version table.
Global defaults remain hardened; schema-specific grants are recreated on upgrade.
PostgreSQL 18 supplies gen_random_uuid(), so no pgcrypto extension is needed
(backend.md 7.6, overview.md 6.3).

The default integration run uses testcontainers postgres:18 and mounts the real
infra role bootstrap. No existing database is used:

    uv run pytest -m integration tests/integration/test_migrations.py

Without Docker, an explicitly disposable PostgreSQL 18 database named test_*
can be supplied through TEST_POSTGRES_URL. Bootstrap it with the same infra
script first, using the test-only passwords in test_migrations.py. Tests remove
all four application schemas; never point this variable at a shared database.

Revisions keep their own table definitions. Current desired metadata lives in
shared_kernel/infrastructure/schema.py, which env.py imports for drift detection.
Future module migrations must add their metadata and replaceable entities to
env.py. Audit function audit_v1.py is frozen once merged; add a versioned function
and a new revision for later changes. attach_audit(table, schema='core') attaches
its PGTrigger; register audit_trigger(table, schema) in env.py for comparison.
Only tables with a UUID id column can use this helper. The idempotency response
cache has a composite key and is not audited.

Functions, views and triggers participate in alembic check. Grants are managed
explicitly in migration SQL and checked by role/privilege integration tests.
ReplaceablePolicy provides create/drop/replace for revision-owned RLS definitions;
downgrade restores the previous definition. No application RLS policy is added
by these foundation migrations (T0204).
